    <DataNode elementId="AT1G31220-MONOMER" textLabel="phosphoribosylglycinamide formyltransferase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="P52422" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="355" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The cDNA encoding the glycinamide ribonucleotide transformylase (GAR transformylase) that catalyzes the conversion of phosphoribosyl glycineamide to phosphoribosyl N-formylglycineamide has been isolated from &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. The molecular function of the enzyme was confirmed by recombinant expression in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; and functional complementation of a GAR transformylase mutant &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; strain. The GAR transformylase has been confirmed to be N10-formyltetrahydrofolate-dependent, which appears to be the plant specific enzyme in contrast to the additional enzyme that can employ formate being found exclusively in bacteria |CITS: [7920700]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G31220-MONOMER" />
